<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victor Hugo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Victor Hugo was a French poet, novelist and dramatist, a leading figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> and author of Notre-Dame de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Les Misérables. An opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s coup of 1851, he went into exile, living in Jersey from 1852 until his expulsion in 1855 and then in Guernsey until 1870. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> and was buried in the Panthéon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-hugo.jpg" alt="Victor Hugo" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Étienne Carjat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Hugo_by_%C3%89tienne_Carjat_1876_-_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
